---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import { lookup } from '@lm15/lm15';
import { LANGUAGES, PROVIDERS, SETUP } from '../data/docs-examples';

interface Props { step: 'install' | 'auth' }
const { step } = Astro.props;
---

{step === 'install' ? (
  <div data-doc-install>
    <div data-doc-install-language="python">
      <p>You’ll need Python 3.10 or newer. Run this in your terminal:</p>
      <Code code="python3 -m pip install --pre lm15" lang="bash" />
      <p>The Python core has no required third-party dependencies: it uses Python’s
        standard library. The <code>--pre</code> flag includes prereleases, so you
        get the API used in this guide rather than the older stable release.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>Using Windows, or keeping this project separate?</summary>
        <p>On Windows, use <code>py</code> instead of <code>python3</code>.</p>
        <p>A virtual environment keeps this project’s packages separate from your
          other Python work. Create one before installing LM15:</p>
        <Code code="python3 -m venv .venv" lang="bash" />
        <p>Activate it on macOS or Linux:</p>
        <Code code="source .venv/bin/activate" lang="bash" />
        <p>Or in Windows PowerShell:</p>
        <Code code=".venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1" lang="powershell" />
        <p>Then install inside it:</p>
        <Code code="python -m pip install --pre lm15" lang="bash" />
      </details>
    </div>
    <div data-doc-install-language="typescript" hidden>
      <p>You’ll need Node.js 22.18 or newer. In a new folder, run this in your terminal:</p>
      <Code code={"npm init -y\nnpm pkg set type=module\nnpm install @lm15/lm15"} lang="bash" />
      <p>LM15 for TypeScript has no runtime dependencies. This installs the current
        release candidate, <code>1.0.0-rc.1</code>, the version this guide uses.
        Save each example in a file ending in <code>.ts</code>, such as <code>first.ts</code>, and run it with <code>node first.ts</code>: Node runs
        TypeScript files directly.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>Adding LM15 to a project you already have?</summary>
        <p>Run only the last command, <code>npm install @lm15/lm15</code>. The
          examples use <code>await</code> at the top level of the file, which needs <code>"type": "module"</code> in your <code>package.json</code> (that’s what
          the second command sets) or a file ending in <code>.mts</code>.</p>
        <p>In a web page, import <code>@lm15/lm15/browser</code> instead; see the <a href="https://github.com/lm15-dev/lm15-ts#readme">TypeScript SDK guide</a>.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div data-doc-install-language="rust" hidden>
      <p>You’ll need a recent Rust toolchain. Run this in your terminal:</p>
      <Code code={"cargo new first-request\ncd first-request\ncargo add lm15\ncargo add tokio --features macros,rt-multi-thread"} lang="bash" />
      <p>Cargo picks the current release candidate, <code>1.0.0-rc.1</code>, because
        there’s no stable 1.0 yet; it’s the version this guide uses. Tokio runs the
        examples’ <code>async</code> code. Put each example in <code>src/main.rs</code> and run it with <code>cargo run</code>.</p>
      <details>
        <summary>An example needs another crate?</summary>
        <p>A few later examples use more crates, or more Tokio features. Each
          example’s first comment names them, for example <code>// Dependencies: lm15, tokio (macros, rt-multi-thread, time)</code>.
          Add what’s missing the same way:</p>
        <Code code="cargo add tokio --features time" lang="bash" />
      </details>
    </div>
    <div data-doc-install-language="go" hidden>
      <p>You’ll need Go 1.26 or newer. Run this in your terminal:</p>
      <Code code={"mkdir first-request && cd first-request\ngo mod init example.com/first-request\ngo get github.com/lm15-dev/lm15-go"} lang="bash" />
      <p>LM15 for Go uses only Go’s standard library. It has no tagged release yet,
        so <code>go get</code> takes the current code and records its exact version
        in <code>go.mod</code> (it looks like <code>v0.0.0-2026…</code>); your
        build stays on that version until you run <code>go get</code> again. Put
        each example in <code>main.go</code> and run it with <code>go run .</code></p>
    </div>
    <div data-doc-install-language="julia" hidden>
      <p>You’ll need Julia 1.10 or newer. In a new folder, run this in your terminal:</p>
      <Code code={"julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/lm15-dev/lm15-jl\")'"} lang="bash" />
      <p>LM15 for Julia isn’t in Julia’s package registry yet, so this installs it
        from GitHub into the folder’s own environment. Save each example as <code>first.jl</code> and run it with <code>julia --project=. first.jl</code>.
        The first run compiles LM15, which takes a minute.</p>
    </div>
    {LANGUAGES.filter(language => !['python', 'typescript', 'rust', 'go', 'julia'].includes(language.id)).map(language => (
      <div data-doc-install-language={language.id} hidden>
        <p><a href={SETUP[language.id].href}>{SETUP[language.id].text}</a></p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
) : (
  <div data-doc-key-setup>
    <p>You only need a key from <strong>one provider</strong>. Choose your provider
      at the top left of the page, then open its key page below. You may need to
      create an account and add credit.</p>
    <ul>
      {PROVIDERS.map(provider => (
        <li data-doc-key-provider={provider.id}>
          <a href={lookup(provider.id)?.consoleUrl}>{provider.label} API keys</a>
          {' — '}<code>{provider.env}</code>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>On macOS or Linux, run the command below in the terminal where you’ll run
      your code. Replace <code>your-api-key</code> with your own key.</p>
    <p data-doc-key-prompt>Choose a provider above to fill in the variable name below.</p>
    <pre class="lm-code"><code data-doc-key-posix>export YOUR_PROVIDER_API_KEY="your-api-key"</code></pre>
    <details>
      <summary>First time setting an environment variable?</summary>
      <p>An environment variable is a named value your terminal passes to the
        programs it starts. LM15’s router reads the key from that value, so you
        don’t need to put it in your code.</p>
      <p>The command above works in Bash and Zsh, the usual shells on macOS and
        Linux. In Windows PowerShell, use:</p>
      <pre class="lm-code"><code data-doc-key-powershell>$env:YOUR_PROVIDER_API_KEY = "your-api-key"</code></pre>
      <p>In Windows Command Prompt, use:</p>
      <pre class="lm-code"><code data-doc-key-cmd>set YOUR_PROVIDER_API_KEY=your-api-key</code></pre>
      <p>These commands apply to the current terminal. Run your example from that
        same terminal; set the key again when you open a new one. An already-open
        notebook or editor won’t automatically pick it up.</p>
      <p>Keep the key private. Don’t include it in shared code, screenshots, or
        version control, and don’t print it to check whether setup worked.</p>
    </details>
    <p data-doc-key-cost>Running the example sends a real request to your chosen
      provider and may cost money. Choose a model your account can access.</p>
    <div data-doc-python-auth>
      <p>Using local Ollama instead? The Python router accepts
        <code>ollama:your-model</code> without an API key. Ollama must be running
        with that model installed; edit the model name in your copied code.</p>
      <p>For explicit keys, rotating credentials, or local Claude and Codex logins,
        see <a href="https://lm15-dev.github.io/lm15-python/authentication/">Python authentication</a>.</p>
      <details data-doc-key-error hidden>
        <summary>Forgot to set the key?</summary>
        <p>Python reports which provider needs a key and which variable to set.
          For your selected provider, the message begins:</p>
        <pre class="lm-code"><code data-doc-key-error-source></code></pre>
        <p>Set the named variable, then run your code again from the same terminal.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
)}

<style>
  [hidden] { display: none !important; }
  details { margin-block: 1rem; }
  summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; }
  pre.lm-code { overflow-x: auto; white-space: pre; overflow-wrap: normal; padding: .75rem 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--sl-color-gray-5); font-size: var(--sl-text-code, .875rem); }
</style>
